TI  - Hybrid MCTS++: An Adaptive Optimizer for Enhanced Game-Playing Performance
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 2nd International Conference on Computational Intelligence Approaches and Applications (ICCIAA)
SP  - 1
EP  - 5
DO  - 10.1109/ICCIAA68481.2026.11543971
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1109/ICCIAA68481.2026.11543971
AB  - Model-based reinforcement learning combines neural networks with planning to achieve superhuman performance in board and video games. MuZero exemplifies this by learning a model and using Monte Carlo tree search (MCTS) for planning. However, its search component employs unstable prior normalization, stochastic action selection, and a fixed simulation budget, all of which limit performance and data efficiency, particularly in low-data regimes. We propose a hybrid optimization method called Hybrid MCTS++, which improves the search element of MuZero. The method computes numerically stable priors using a masked softmax over legal actions, deterministically selects actions by taking the maximum UCB score, and adapts the number of simulations according to the entropy of the root policy distribution. This adaptive schedule allocates more simulations when the search is uncertain and fewer when the model is confident. We mathematically formulate the algorithm, provide pseudocode, and evaluate it in the Tic-Tac-Toe environment included in the MuZero repository. Against a random opponent, our hybrid search achieved 100% accuracy and sensitivity, improving the baseline accuracy of MCTS of 74% accuracy and 69.8% sensitivity. Against an expert opponent, Hybrid MCTS++ reached 86% accuracy and 36.4% sensitivity compared to baseline’s 22% accuracy and 2.5% sensitivity. These results demonstrate that Hybrid MCTS++ markedly enhances search efficiency and predictive metrics without retraining underlying models, offering a simple yet powerful upgrade for model-based reinforcement learning systems overall.
