AB  - Model-based reinforcement learning hinges on the fidelity of the learned dynamics, yet driving down predictive bias can trigger large inter-iteration model shifts that derail policy improvement. We derive a Wasserstein policy-improvement lower bound that ties monotonic return gains to a combined error term capturing model bias and model shift, providing theory-backed guarantees under bounded combined error. This leads to Dynamic Weight Constraints for Model-Based Policy Optimization method(DWC-PO), a model-learning objective with adaptive weights that emphasize accuracy early and penalize shifts when they threaten improvement—without hand-tuned schedules. DWC-PO operationalizes the bound to produce reliable model updates and stable policy learning across diverse MBRL settings. Experiments show higher sample efficiency and reduced variance than strong baselines, with ablations confirming the necessity of explicitly controlling the combined error.
