AB  - Medical image analysis, especially to aid in brain and spine diagnostics, is a difficult task because of the large variability in the structure of these organs in the body, the presence of imaging artifacts, and the lack of available labeled expert annotations. Current deep-learning approaches, which are achieving promising results, are usually highly dependent on large supervised datasets and incorporate no explicit reasoning mechanisms for multi-step diagnostic decision making. In this manuscript, we propose Med-MuZero, a novel model-based reinforcement learning (RL) framework developed based on the MuZero algorithm, which has demonstrated superhuman performance in mastering games without prior knowledge of rules. Med-MuZero learns a latent dynamics model to simulate diagnostic trajectories, predict pathology probabilities, intermediate confidence scores, and next-step imaging features. Evaluated on two publicly available datasets—BrainMRI (10,000 T1-weighted scans) and SpineXray (8,500 radiography images of the human spine)—our approach achieves state-of-the-art classification accuracies of 99.73% and 99.81% for brain tumor detection and spinal fracture identification, respectively, outperforming leading CNN and transformer-based models by significant margins. The combination of search-based planning and a learned model for diagnosis provides the transparency of multi-hypothesis reasoning, thereby bridging the gap between high-performing game AI and clinical decision support systems. Our results demonstrate the potential of advanced RL paradigms to deliver explainable and highly accurate medical image analysis tools.
