AB  - By constructing environment dynamics models, model-based reinforcement learning (MBRL) is able to significantly improve sample efficiency. However, under high-dimensional image observations, traditional MBRL methods are limited by the noise and redundant information contained in input images, making it difficult to accurately model environment dynamics, which in turn restricts the model’s generalization ability and stability. To address this issue, this article explicitly integrates multifrequency-enhanced image features with original spatial domain features and proposes a Twin World Model assisted by multifrequency (MF-TWM)-enhanced image features, aiming to improve the robustness of state representations to spectral variations and the accuracy of state predictions. First, multifrequency-enhanced images are generated via Fourier transform and a stochastic filter bank to capture diverse frequency-domain information. Then, a twin-structured architecture with shared parameters is introduced to process the original and multifrequency-enhanced images as two parallel views. In addition, a Gaussian-weighted cross-view contrastive loss and a temporal distance-based weighting mechanism are designed to encourage consistent latent dynamics across the two views. Finally, to effectively model complex spatiotemporal dependencies, a hybrid world model structure based on Mamba2-Transformer is proposed, which combines the strength of state-space models (SSMs) in capturing long-term dependencies with the sensitivity of Transformer to local dynamic variations. Experiments conducted on the Atari 100 K benchmark demonstrate that the proposed method achieves significant improvements over existing mainstream MBRL baselines in both sample efficiency and final episodic return.
