AB  - Strategic decision making has been one of the central themes in security informatics research. Automated planning and reinforcement learning (RL) as the two main forms of approaches to solving decision-making problems, can facilitate a wide range of strategic representation, analytics, prediction and management tasks in security-related applications. Despite the remarkable advancements of deep RL, it suffers from low sample efficiency and convergence issues in sparsereward environments. Traditionally, planning can provide the high-level guidance to address the limitations of deep RL, yet typically relies on the hand-crafted domain model construction that causes a major bottleneck. With the rapid development of large language models (LLMs), the potential of LLMs offers a new alternative to automate domain model construction for strategic planning. To facilitate strategic decision making in the security domain, in this paper, we focus on releasing the capability of LLMs to construct hierarchical plan representations from the natural language descriptions of planning domain, and design a generic LLM-enabled framework LLM-HPRL to integrate hierarchical planning with deep RL. Specifically, we first develop a two-stage LLM-based domain model generation process to automatically construct hierarchical plan representations, including domain actions, their preconditions, effects, and hierarchical task structure. Then a hierarchical planner is employed to generate feasible plans, which are bridged by symbolic options to provide high-level guidance and global structure for enhancing deep RL. We conduct experiments based on three strategic games from Gym-Minigrid environments, and the results verify the efficacy of our framework for enhancing deep RL in sparse-reward scenarios.
