AB  - Reinforcement Learning (RL) has emerged as a promising approach for motion control of quadruped and humanoid robots, but excessive contact force during locomotion remains a critical issue that compromises operational safety and generates unwanted motion noise. This paper proposes a novel reward function optimization framework for reinforcement learning, aiming to mitigate contact force and reduce motion noise while maintaining locomotion stability and efficiency. The optimized reward function integrates three core components: contact force constraint reward, smooth motion reward, and stability maintenance reward. Comparative experiments are conducted on both quadruped and humanoid robot models in simulated environments (PyBullet 3.2.5 and ROS Gazebo 11). Results show that the proposed reward modification reduces peak contact force by 30% (quadruped, flat ground), with an average reduction of 20% across all terrains, decreases motion noise by 29.3% (quadruped) in root-mean-square (RMS) intensity, and maintains gait stability margin above 0.12m (quadruped) and 0.08m (humanoid) (vs. traditional RL: 0.11m and 0.07m). Meanwhile, locomotion speed decreases by only 10% relative to the traditional RL controller. This research provides an effective solution for improving the safety and environmental adaptability of legged robots in practical applications.
