AB  - Aiming at the challenges of limited target prior information, active evading games, constrained sonar detection, and velocity lag of tracking platforms in complex underwater dynamic antagonistic environments, this paper proposes an integrated “perception-game-control” cooperative search and hunting decision-making framework for unmanned underwater vehicle (UUV) swarms. To address the primary novelty of this study, first, during the search phase, a dynamic game-based underwater swarm search decision (DG-USSD) algorithm is proposed. By constructing an initial target distribution based on task scenarios, this algorithm iteratively updates the target's probability map utilizing Bayesian optimization and Markov state transition matrices, transforming passive search into an active two-way dynamic game for rolling path planning. Second, during the hunting phase, a state-prediction-driven and geometric-bias-guided swarm encirclement (SPGB-SE) hunting algorithm is proposed. An adaptive-weight interactive multiple model extended Kalman filter (AW-IMM-EKF) is introduced to accurately estimate and predict the target's trajectory. Concurrently, an active predictive bias mechanism based on Apollonius geometry is designed to guide the swarm to intercept the target in advance, breaking the physical constraint requiring pursuers to be faster than the evader. Simulation results demonstrate that, compared with conventional cooperative methods, the proposed framework improves the target search success rate by 21.88% and the hunting success rate by 17.21%. As a major practical implication, this work provides a robust decision-making mechanism for UUV swarms operating under severe velocity lag and acoustic constraints in hostile underwater environments.
