AB  - Generating correct and flexible models of realistic, partially unknown worlds is an old goal in artificial intelligence. Developments in representation learning, generative modelling, inventions of self-supervised methods mean that AI systems have attained inner models of the world that allow them to perform nearly the same as human perception regarding real-world dynamics without direct supervision. In this paper, an innovative training scheme to train AI agents to anticipate a reality by learning self-built portrayal of their environment is covered. Using variational autoencoders and generative adversarial networks, our method allows agents to learn underlying structure and predict future states and in addition to being able to represent a large number of complex realistic scenarios. We experimentally analyse the efficacy of this approach in the domains of synthetic and physical preparations that reinforce the realization of agents outfitted with self-built world models giving better generalization, predictive precision, and utilization of resources. With references to experimental results, the models are likely to contribute to increasing real-time decision making, autonomous control as well as creation of virtual environment in a wide selection of practical applications. Lastly, we mention some of the main limitations such as representation fidelity, scalability, and computational complexity and future directions depending on which simulations could be made to use better representations and achieve results that could be deployed in dynamic real-world environments more easily.
