EP  - 799
DO  - 10.1109/ICETCI68937.2026.11610699
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1109/ICETCI68937.2026.11610699
AB  - Visual reinforcement learning faces core problems such as low sample efficiency, disconnect between visual features and dynamic modeling, low credibility of virtual samples, and accumulation of dynamic prediction errors, making it difficult to meet the requirements of engineering deployment. This paper proposes a world model FD-EMWM that integrates visual feature decoupling and dynamic error correction to specifically optimize sample efficiency in visual reinforcement learning. The algorithm consists of four modules: visual feature decoupling, dynamic modeling and error correction, virtual sample generation, and policy optimization. By improving the self-supervised dual-branch network, redundant environmental features and core task features are separated. The Transformer architecture and real-time error correction unit suppress the accumulation of prediction errors. GAN is used to generate virtual visual samples and a screening mechanism is set to ensure sample quality. An Actor-Critic architecture is used to achieve joint training of real and virtual samples. Comparative experiments were conducted on 21 tasks across three benchmark environments: Atari 100 k, DMControl, and Meta-World. The results show that FD-EMWM improves sample efficiency by 35.1%–78.6% and decision performance by 15.8%–19.8% compared to mainstream baseline algorithms such as DRQ V2, DreamerV3, and EfficientZero V2. It also exhibits better training stability and computational efficiency, effectively addressing the core shortcomings of existing algorithms and providing efficient and stable technical support for the practical application of visual reinforcement learning.
