UR  - https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/stamp/stamp.jsp?arnumber=11707562
AB  - Digitizing Piping and Instrumentation Diagrams (P&IDs) is a critical step in modernizing industrial automation systems. Among the most challenging aspects of this process is the reliable detection and classification of symbols, which are often represented with high variability in shape, scale, and context. This variability, combined with surrounding visual artifacts, inconsistent text annotations, and the absence of large-scale annotated datasets, limits the applicability of traditional supervised machine learning approaches. Recent advancements in open-world object detection offer promising avenues to overcome these limitations. Models trained on large semi-supervised datasets become expressive enough to capture the low-level semantics of technical diagrams, even though the training data differs drastically to the symbols we seek to detect. Although numerous research groups in both industry and academia are actively working on solutions for P&ID digitization, existing approaches often face limitations in terms of generalizability, robustness, and scalability. Our work systematically positions these developments and addresses key limitations through a methodological approach. In this work, we evaluate the performance of four state-of-the-art open-world models (OWL, OWL-v2, SAM2, and YOLO-World) on the task of symbol detection within P&ID diagrams. Our analysis focuses on several key dimensions: detection accuracy, bounding box quality, symbol coverage, and sensitivity to hyperparameter configurations. We conduct experiments across a diverse set of P&ID examples to assess each model’s robustness and generalization capabilities. The results reveal distinct strengths and trade-offs among the models, with implications for their integration into scalable digitization pipelines, such as ABB’s “Engineering Data Funnel” (EDF). We establish which models demonstrate strong semantic generalization, which excel in segmentation precision, and what model offers the most compelling balance between speed and adaptability. Our findings suggest that open-world detectors represent a promising baseline for future work in industrial document understanding, particularly in symbol-heavy domains like P&IDs. We conclude by outlining future directions for dataset development, model fine-tuning, and hybrid approaches that combine detection with domain-specific heuristics
