AB  - Summary <p>This chapter discusses the future directions of computational experiment method. As a core methodology connecting social science and intelligent science, computational experimentation plays a crucial role in reconciling the cognitive tensions between the two disciplines—social science emphasizes explaining processes, while intelligent science focuses more on predicting outcomes. Building a foundational scientific theory system for AI4SS computational experiments requires solving three core problems: real‐world model mapping, experimental design and causal analysis, and extrapolation validation of virtual conclusions. The chapter presents significant differences between generative simulation and traditional simulation. Data‐driven generative simulation technology holds tremendous potential in the field of computational experiments. It not only promotes advancements in robotics through efficient and intelligent simulation capabilities but also offers new solutions for virtual reality, game development, and related domains. Traditional modeling methods face inherent trade‐offs between realism, interpretability, and computational efficiency.</p>
