AB  - Edge computing addresses high latency, bandwidth consumption, and privacy leakage in cloud computing. However, the mobility of mobile devices leads to dynamic changes in network topology and instability of wireless channels, posing significant challenges to formulating efficient and reliable offloading strategies. To tackle these challenges, Digital Twin (DT) technology is proposed to map the physical world in virtual space, thereby facilitating prediction of dynamic environments to support optimization of task offloading decisions. Nevertheless, maintaining real-time synchronization between digital twins and physical entities incurs substantial communication overhead and energy consumption of mobile devices, leading to inefficient task execution in resource-constrained edge environments. Therefore, we innovatively construct a novel non-real-time updated DT-assisted dynamic edge computing network framework. This framework is the first to introduce a non-real-time update strategy to balance the fidelity of twin data and system overhead. Under this framework, we investigate a Precision-Delay-Energy Balance Optimization (PDEBO) problem, optimizing the update frequency of the DT, the offloading decisions, resource allocation to minimize the system's overall comprehensive performance including the estimation deviation of DT, task delay, and energy consumption. To solve the problem, we first design a three-layer Computation offloading and adaptive DT Update frequency Optimization (CDTUO) algorithm, which decomposes the problem and gradually approximates the optimal solution through an alternating iteration approach. Further, we propose a Frequency-Offload-Resource Optimization algorithm based on Hierarchical Deep Reinforcement Learning (FORO-HDRL), which enables efficient decision-making while ensuring real-time requirements. Then, extensive experimental results demonstrate that our proposed non-real-time update strategy outperforms traditional real-time strategies.
